Recall that a variational autoencoder (VAE) is an infinite mixture model using the identity:
$$\begin{aligned}
    p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}) = \int_{\mathbf{z}} p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x} \mid \mathbf{z})p_{\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{z})d\mathbf{z}
\end{aligned}$$
Analogously, any conditional distribution can be defined as the marginal over some latent variable:
$$\begin{aligned}
    p_{\theta}(\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{x}) = \int_{\mathbf{z}} p_{\theta}(\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{z}, \mathbf{x})p_{\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{z} \mid \mathbf{x})d\mathbf{z}
\end{aligned}$$
In conditional VAEs (cVAEs), we restrict latent variables $\mathbf{z}$ to be independent of the inputs we condition on, so:
$$\begin{aligned}
    p_{\theta}(\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{x}) = \int_{\mathbf{z}} p_{\theta}(\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{z}, \mathbf{x})p_{\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{z})d\mathbf{z} \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad\text{cVAE likelihood model}
\end{aligned}$$

This corresponds to this graphical model:
[FIGURE:184e45370a8359cb | A directed acyclic graph (DAG) representing a conditional generative model. It consists of three circular nodes: $X$ and $Z$ at the top, and $Y$ at the bottom. Directed arrows point from $X$ to $Y$...]

The idea is that $\mathbf{z}$ encodes information about $\mathbf{y}$ that is independent from what $\mathbf{x}$ already tells us about $\mathbf{y}$. For example, to predict the motion of a billiard ball in a video, given a frame $\mathbf{x}$ and asked to predict the next frame $\mathbf{y}$, we know the ball's color and rough position from $\mathbf{x}$ but not its velocity. The missing information is velocity, which $\mathbf{z}$ can encode. If the model interprets $\mathbf{z}$ as velocity, then $p_{\theta}(\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{z}, \mathbf{x})$ can perfectly predict the next frame, and marginalizing over $\mathbf{z}$ places max likelihood on the data.

This is what tends to happen in a cVAE, though not guaranteed.
@fig-conditional_generative_models-cVAE_ball_bouncing_example shows this scenario.

(see figure: A scenario where a yellow ball is moving across a plane. The observation, $\mathbf{x}$, is a static frame. From that observation, we know what will be the color and rough position of the ball in the...)

Like regular VAEs, cVAEs have an encoder that predicts the optimal importance sampling distribution $p_{\theta}(Z \mid \mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y})$. In practice, cVAEs are trained like regular VAEs except the encoder takes $\mathbf{x}$ and $\mathbf{y}$ as input, and the decoder takes $\mathbf{x}$ and $\mathbf{z}$ as input.
@fig-conditional_generative_models-cVAE_ball_bouncing_example_nets depicts this setting.

(see figure: cVAE architecture. The dotted lines indicate that the *target encoder* is only used during training; at test time, usage follows the solid path.)
